(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_molecularbasisofinheritance=[{id:"neet-2026-q104",pn:104,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Transcription unit",q:`Which of the following statements are correct with reference to a transcription unit?
A. A transcription unit in DNA is defined primarily by three regions : promoter, structural gene and terminator.
B. The promoter is said to be located towards the 5\u2032-end of the structural gene.
C. The promoter is a DNA sequence that provides binding site for RNA polymerase.
D. The promoter defines the template and coding strands.
E. The terminator is located towards the 3\u2032-end of the coding strand and it defines the end of the process of transcription.`,o:["A, B, C, D and E","B, C, D and E only","A, C, D and E only","A, B, C and D only"],c:0,s:`The promoter and terminator flank the structural gene in a transcription unit.
The promoter is located towards the 5\u2032-end of the structural gene.
It is a DNA sequence that gives the binding site for RNA polymerase, and its presence also defines the template and coding strands.
The terminator is located towards the 3\u2032-end of the coding strand and usually defines the end of transcription.
So all of A, B, C, D and E are correct.`},{id:"neet-2026-q116",pn:116,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"DNA fingerprinting",q:`Arrange the following steps of DNA fingerprinting in a correct sequence.
A. Isolation of DNA and its digestion by restriction endonucleases.
B. Hybridisation using a labelled VNTR probe.
C. Transferring of separated DNA fragments to synthetic membranes.
D. Detection of hybridised DNA fragments by autoradiography.
E. Separation of DNA fragments by electrophoresis.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A, E, C, B, D","A, E, B, C, D","A, B, D, C, E","A, D, B, E, C"],c:0,s:`Correct order of DNA fingerprinting:
A. Isolate DNA and cut it with restriction endonuclease.
E. Separate the fragments by size using gel electrophoresis.
C. Transfer the separated fragments to synthetic membranes of nylon or nitrocellulose.
B. Let probes complementary to the VNTR locus hybridise with the fragments.
D. Finally detect the hybridised fragments under X-rays by autoradiography.
So A, E, C, B, D.`},{id:"neet-2026-q123",pn:123,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Packaging of DNA helix",q:`Which of the following statements are correct with reference to packaging of DNA helix?
A. Histones are organized to form a unit of eight molecules called histone octamer.
B. Histones are negatively charged basic proteins.
C. Histones are rich in the basic amino acid residues - lysine and arginine.
D. The positively charged DNA is wrapped around the histone octamer to form nucleosome.
E. The packaging of chromatin at higher levels requires an additional set of proteins called non-histone chromosomal proteins.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A, B and D only","A, C and E only","C, D and E only","B, D and E only"],c:1,s:`Eight histones combine to form a histone octamer.
Histones are positively charged basic proteins, rich in lysine and arginine.
The negatively charged DNA is wrapped around the histone octamer to form a nucleosome.
Packaging of chromatin at higher levels needs NHC (non-histone chromosomal) proteins.
So A, C and E are correct; B and D are wrong.`},{id:"neet-2026-q125",pn:125,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Lac operon",q:"In the lac operon, the z gene codes for",o:["transacetylase","the repressor of lac operon","permease","beta-galactosidase"],c:3,s:`In the lac operon:
i gene codes for the regulator (repressor) protein.
z gene codes for beta-galactosidase.
y gene codes for permease.
a gene codes for transacetylase.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q163",pn:163,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Lac operon",q:"Which of the following statements about lac-operon is correct?",o:["Lactose activates repressor to bind to the operator","Genes i, z, y and a share single common promoter","Galactose can act as an inducer of lac operon","Gene i is constitutively expressed"],c:3,s:`Galactose cannot act as an inducer of lac operon.
Gene i is constitutively expressed in lac operon.
The repressor binds to the operator when lactose is not available.
Genes z, y and a are structural genes and have a different promoter from that of gene i.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q180",pn:180,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Transcription",q:"Which of the following enzymes synthesizes precursor mRNA?",o:["RNA polymerase II","RNA polymerase III","DNA polymerase","RNA polymerase I"],c:0,s:"RNA polymerase II transcribes the precursor of mRNA, the heterogeneous nuclear RNA (hnRNA)."},{id:"neet-2025-q106",pn:106,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Human genome",q:"Which chromosome in the human genome has the highest number of genes?",o:["Chromosome X","Chromosome Y","Chromosome 1","Chromosome 10"],c:2,s:"In the human genome, chromosome 1 has the highest number of genes, i.e., 2968."},{id:"neet-2025-q114",pn:114,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"RNA types and RNAi",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Transfer RNAs and ribosomal RNA do not interact with mRNA.
Statement II : RNA interference (RNAi) takes place in all eukaryotic organisms as a method of cellular defence.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both statement I and statement II are correct","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct"],c:3,s:`Both transfer RNAs and ribosomal RNA interact with mRNA, so Statement I is incorrect.
RNA interference (RNAi) takes place in all eukaryotic organisms as a method of cellular defence, so Statement II is correct.`},{id:"neet-2025-q129",pn:129,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Transcription and RNA processing",q:`Which of the following are the post-transcriptional events in an eukaryotic cell?
A. Transport of pre-mRNA to cytoplasm prior to splicing.
B. Removal of introns and joining of exons.
C. Addition of methyl group at 5' end of hnRNA.
D. Addition of adenine residues at 3' end of hnRNA.
E. Base pairing of two complementary RNAs.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A, B, C only","B, C, D only","B, C, E only","C, D, E only"],c:1,s:`Transcription is copying of genetic information from one strand of DNA into RNA.
Transport of pre-mRNA to cytoplasm prior to splicing is not a post-transcriptional event.
The primary transcript becomes functional mRNA after post-transcriptional processing in 3 steps:
Modification of 5' end by capping.
Tailing.
Splicing.
Base pairing of two complementary RNAs is not a post-transcriptional event.
Hence B, C, D are post-transcriptional events in a eukaryotic cell.`},{id:"neet-2025-q134",pn:134,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"DNA as genetic material",q:`Match List I with List II:
A. Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase \u2014 I. Streptococcus pneumoniae
B. Euchromatin \u2014 II. Densely packed and dark-stained
C. Frederick Griffith \u2014 III. Loosely packed and light-stained
D. Heterochromatin \u2014 IV. DNA as genetic material confirmation
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I"],c:2,s:`Proof that DNA is the genetic material came from the experiment of Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase (A-IV).
Euchromatin is the lightly stained region with loosely packed chromatin fibre (B-III).
Frederick Griffith did experiments with different strains of Streptococcus pneumoniae (C-I).
Heterochromatin is the darkly stained region with tightly packed chromatin fibre (D-II).`},{id:"neet-2025-q163",pn:163,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Genetic code",q:"Who proposed that the genetic code for amino acids should be made up of three nucleotides?",o:["George Gamow","Francis Crick","Jacque Monod","Franklin Stahl"],c:0,s:"George Gamow, a physicist, proposed that the genetic code for amino acids should be made up of three nucleotides."},{id:"neet-2025-q174",pn:174,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Transcription",q:"Which factor is important for termination of transcription?",o:["\u03B1 (alpha)","\u03C3 (sigma)","\u03C1 (rho)","\u03B3 (gamma)"],c:2,s:`In prokaryotes the RNA polymerase can only catalyse elongation.
It associates for a short time with the initiation factor (\u03C3) and the termination factor (\u03C1), to start and to end transcription respectively.`},{id:"neet-2024-q125",pn:125,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Transcription unit",q:"A transcription unit in DNA is defined primarily by the three regions in DNA and these are with respect to upstream and down stream end;",o:["Inducer, Repressor, Structural gene","Promotor, Structural gene, Terminator","Repressor, Operator gene, Structural gene","Structural gene, Transposons, Operator gene"],c:1,s:`A transcription unit in DNA is defined by three regions:
(i) a promoter,
(ii) the structural gene,
(iii) a terminator.
The promoter lies towards the 5' end (upstream) of the structural gene, with reference to the coding strand.
The terminator lies towards the 3' end (downstream) of the coding strand.`},{id:"neet-2024-q129",pn:129,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Lac operon",q:"The lactose present in the growth medium of bacteria is transported to the cell by the action of",o:["Permease","Polymerase","Beta-galactosidase","Acetylase"],c:0,s:`The y gene of the lac operon codes for permease.
Permease increases the permeability of the cell to \u03B2-galactosides.
So lactose from the medium enters the cell through the action of permease.`},{id:"neet-2024-q140",pn:140,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Scientists and discoveries",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List I \u2014 List II
A. Frederick Griffith \u2014 I. Genetic code
B. Francois Jacob & Jacque Monod \u2014 II. Semi-conservative mode of DNA replication
C. Har Gobind Khorana \u2014 III. Transformation
D. Meselson & Stahl \u2014 IV. Lac operon
Choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],c:3,s:`Frederick Griffith's experiments showed transformation in bacteria.
Francois Jacob and Jacques Monod worked out the lac operon.
Meselson and Stahl showed the semi-conservative mode of DNA replication.
Har Gobind Khorana developed a chemical method to define the base combinations of the genetic code.
So A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.`},{id:"neet-2024-q145",pn:145,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"DNA replication",q:"Which of the following statement is correct regarding the process of replication in E.coli ?",o:["The DNA dependent DNA polymerase catalyses polymerization in 5'\u21923' as well as 3'\u21925' direction.","The DNA dependent DNA polymerase catalyses polymerization in 5'\u21923' direction.","The DNA dependent DNA polymerase catalyses polymerization in one direction, that is 3'\u21925'.","The DNA dependent RNA polymerase catalyase polymerization in one direction, that is 5'\u21923'."],c:1,s:"In prokaryotes such as E. coli, DNA dependent DNA polymerase catalyses polymerisation in only one direction, 5' \u2192 3'."}]})();
